## 5. Consent and rights — this is where a fashion crossover gets dangerous

Student design work is **authored creative property**. Nothing here is covered by the FE consent set alone. Fashion Design (CLO) and CD students need the same rights regime.

| # | Consent | Whose | Nature |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **S1** | Exhibiting a design in vShowroom | CD **and** Diseño de Moda student | Per-artefact, revocable, licence stated (author retains copyright; platform gets display licence only) |
| **S2** | Attribution vs. anonymity | same | Author's choice; default is attributed, but opt-out must exist |
| **S3** | Research use of the interaction / process data | FE + participating fashion cohorts | Separate from S1/S2; per [`fe-cohort-case-proposal.mdc`](./fe-cohort-case-proposal.mdc) §6 |
| **S4** | Cross-institution data flow | UDIT ↔ UEM | **Two institutions, two regimes.** Needs both DPOs. Minimise: the platform should hold no personal data of visitors in v1 |
| **S5** | CLO asset derivatives (renders, glTF, stills) | Diseño de Moda student | Export formats are still the author's work; state what derivatives the platform may generate |

**Design rule that avoids most of the problem:** v1 stores *pieces and designers*, and "collect/follow" state stays **client-side (localStorage)** unless and until accounts are justified. No accounts, no visitor personal data, no cross-border transfer question.

> If the DPO conversation stalls, **ship v1 with local-only collections.** The product still works and the research still has cohort-level engineering evidence.

