## 6. What each side gets

**FE students** — a real client with real CLO/avatar content, a genuine performance problem (3D assets are heavy), an accessibility problem that matters (can a screen-reader user navigate a 3D showroom?), and a public artefact for their portfolio.

**Diseño de Moda students (UDIT)** — a browser-native exhibition of **CLO 3D** work without depending on proprietary viewers alone; Portfolio / TFG evidence that is live and shareable; a first encounter with the interface layer as something *made*.

**CD students (UEM)** — their avatars and digital looks exhibited on a platform built to professional standards; authentic user role toward FE.

**The professor** — one methodology spanning FE + CD, one shared artefact, and a same-campus fashion-design content stream that makes the fashion-portfolio research gap *empirical* rather than speculative.

**The fields** — a documented attempt at the fashion-portfolio framework gap, from a cohort that has garment authoring (CLO), digital-fashion communication (CD), and interface engineering (FE).

